   "title": "El Sur",
   "year": 1983,
   "rating": 5.0,
   "review": "This film brings Janet Planet to mind. The insights into a child's interiority and lived experience are remarkably delicate—they require not just a profound attunement to life and emotion, but the formal mastery to transmute that sensibility into cinematic language.\r\nJanet Planet captures the density of time: that sticky, seemingly interminable stretch of summer. Boredom, in itself, is a quiet romanticism; you haven't yet learned to measure your existence against objectives.\r\nEl Sur, on the other hand, dwells in spatial darkness—hunting for a source of light under constraint, knowing you can keep living as long as there is any light at all. It is in this exact drift that we grasp how irreducibly complex people are, and slowly become more whole ourselves.",
